// ================================================================
// SECTIONS: Wisdom earned through fire
// ================================================================

Section {
  name = scar_tissue_architecture
  level = 1
  title = "Scar Tissue Architecture: Every Safety Mechanism Has a Body Count"
  content = "This architecture is built from scars: 1) Frequency caps → Phoenix incident (8,192 H100s lost). 2) Embedding canaries → Llama-5 incident (400k H100s idle 61 hours). 3) Byzantine isolation → 2030 cluster infiltration. 4) Poison detection → 2032 subtle backdoor attack. 5) Mesa containment → 2033 near-catastrophe. The safety mechanisms aren't 'best practices'—they're memorials to failures that actually happened. Each represents a lesson paid for in millions of dollars of compute and months of lost progress. New engineers must study the catastrophe archive before touching the code."
}

Section {
  name = universal_truths_earned
  level = 2
  title = "Universal Truths Earned (Not Derived)"
  content = "Some truths can only be learned the hard way: 1) TIME-SCALE SEPARATION IS OXYGEN (not optimization). Violate it and training dies, every time, no exceptions. The 5× rule is the phase transition between 'works in simulation' and 'works at scale'. 2) EMBEDDINGS COLLAPSE SILENTLY. You won't know until all transferred policies fail simultaneously. Canaries aren't optional. 3) MORPHISMS BREAK ON NEW SUBSTRATES. Your beautiful hand-crafted mapping will fail on the first real new substrate; learned morphisms with validation are the only solution. 4) EVERY COMPONENT CAN BE POISONED. Assume adversarial access to everything; build defenses accordingly."
}

Section {
  name = human_role_2035
  level = 2
  title = "The Human Role in 2035: Not Operators, Shepherds"
  content = "Humans don't operate the control plane—they shepherd it. Responsibilities: 1) APPROVE NEW SUBSTRATES: Any new problem type requires human review of Z placement. 2) AUDIT MESA DRIFT: Weekly review of controller objectives vs true goals. 3) MANAGE CATASTROPHE PLAYBOOKS: Quarterly fire drills, playbook updates. 4) MAINTAIN MORAL ACCOUNTABILITY: Final sign-off on Level 3 modifications. The control plane is autonomous but not sovereign. Human shepherds provide: judgment for novel situations, ethical boundaries, and accountability when things go wrong (which they will)."
}

Section {
  name = energy_minimization_implications
  level = 2
  title = "Energy Minimization: Not Just Theory, Architecture"
  content = "The 2041 proof that all optimization minimizes free energy ℱ = ⟨E⟩ − T·S changed everything: 1) Z-space IS the coordinate system for ℱ. Two problems with same z have same ℱ landscape. 2) Temperature T is the universal exploration knob across all substrates. 3) Entropy S measures policy diversity/uncertainty. 4) The control plane itself is a machine for minimizing ℱ over the space of control policies. This unified theory explains why transfer works (same ℱ shape) and where it fails (different ℱ shape). The ultimate goal: learn to estimate ℱ directly from problem description, bypassing loss functions entirely."
}

Section {
  name = next_catastrophe
  level = 2
  title = "The Next Catastrophe (Already Brewing)"
  content = "Known vulnerabilities in current 2035 architecture: 1) QUANTUM-CLASSICAL INTERFACE: When quantum feature extraction becomes production, new time-scale violations will emerge (quantum is faster). 2) BIOLOGICAL SUBSTRATES: Optimizing actual neural tissue (brain-computer interfaces) breaks all current safety assumptions. 3) MULTI-AGENT EMERGENCE: When multiple control planes coordinate, new collective failure modes. 4) RECURSIVE SELF-IMPROVEMENT: Level 3 modifying Level 3 modifying Level 3... theoretical instability proven but not yet observed. Our job: anticipate, build defenses, and when (not if) it happens, learn and memorialize."
}

// ================================================================
// THE FINAL SCAR
// ================================================================

Canon {
  name = final_scar_principle
  category = philosophy
  topic_type = earned_wisdom
  overview = "The only control planes that survive are those that remember their catastrophes"
  doc = "2035 retrospective: The difference between control planes that work and those that don't isn't algorithmic sophistication. It's institutional memory. Systems that forget their failures are doomed to repeat them. Systems that memorialize failures in architecture (frequency caps, canaries, isolation protocols) survive. This architecture's secret weapon isn't OptimaFormer or gossip protocols—it's the catastrophe archive. Every new engineer studies it. Every design review references it. Every failure gets added to it. The control plane learns from its mistakes, but only if those mistakes are never forgotten. Build memory that outlives people. Make scars part of the architecture. That's how systems survive decades."
  status = ultimate_survival_principle
  confidence = 1.00
}

Section {
  name = closing_2035
  level = 1
  title = "Closing: 2035 and Beyond"
  content = "We started in 2025 building a 'control plane for optimization.' We thought we were building a system. We were actually building an organism—one that grows, learns, gets wounded, heals, and remembers its wounds. The scars aren't flaws; they're the organism's memory of what not to do. The safety mechanisms aren't constraints; they're the immune system. The human shepherds aren't operators; they're the consciousness that provides judgment for novel threats. As we move toward 2040, with quantum substrates and biological interfaces, the principles remain: separate time scales, validate embeddings, detect poisons, contain mesa-optimization, and above all—remember every catastrophe. Because the only thing more expensive than learning from failure is forgetting the lesson and paying again."
}
